Fix release WAL persistence for delay 0 (issue #66; upstream bug, unfixed in
v1.13 and on master).

When persistence is enabled (-b <dir>), `release <id> <pri> 0` applies the new
priority and increments the release counter in memory, but OP_RELEASE only
reserved WAL space and passed update_store = !!delay to enqueue_job(), so with
delay 0 walwrite() was skipped entirely. The on-disk binlog kept the stale
pre-release record, and on daemon restart prot_replay restored the job with
its original priority and its reserves and releases counters reset to 0.

Always reserve WAL space via walresvupdate(&c->srv->wal) and always pass
update_store = 1 to enqueue_job(), so every release writes the updated job
record (priority, counters, state, and delay deadline) to the WAL, as bury
already does.

--- a/prot.c
+++ b/prot.c
@@ -1579,22 +1579,20 @@
             return;
         }
 
-        /* We want to update the delay deadline on disk, so reserve space for
-         * that. */
-        if (delay) {
-            int z = walresvupdate(&c->srv->wal);
-            if (!z) {
-                reply_serr(c, MSG_OUT_OF_MEMORY);
-                return;
-            }
-            j->walresv += z;
+        /* We want to update the priority, release count, and delay deadline
+         * on disk, so reserve space for that. */
+        int z = walresvupdate(&c->srv->wal);
+        if (!z) {
+            reply_serr(c, MSG_OUT_OF_MEMORY);
+            return;
         }
+        j->walresv += z;
 
         j->r.pri = pri;
         j->r.delay = delay;
         j->r.release_ct++;
 
-        r = enqueue_job(c->srv, j, delay, !!delay);
+        r = enqueue_job(c->srv, j, delay, 1);
         if (r < 0) {
             reply_serr(c, MSG_INTERNAL_ERROR);
             return;
